This is a common error that occurs when trying to read input using Java's Scanner class. The InputMismatchException is thrown when the next token in the input stream does not match the expected data type (in this case, an integer).

To fix this issue, you can add input validation to ensure that the user enters a valid integer before proceeding with your program logic. Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int number = 0;

        // Prompt user for input and validate it until a valid integer is entered
        while (true) {
            try {
                System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
                number = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining input on the line
            }
        }

        // Use the retrieved input as needed...
        System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
    }
}
```

In this updated version of the code, we create a while loop that will continue prompting the user for input until a valid integer is entered. Inside the loop, we use a try-catch block to handle the InputMismatchException that might be thrown if the user enters non-integer input. If such an exception is caught, we consume any remaining input on the line using `scanner.nextLine()`, so we don't have to worry about it causing issues later on. Once a valid integer is entered, we exit the loop and continue with our program logic.

I hope this helps clarify how to handle input validation in Java! Let me know if you have any further questions or concerns.

